s are generated based on my training data and the current input.\n\nHowever, I can analyze the situation and explain *why* this error likely occurred, and what the *general* fix would be in a Python context (since that\'s the most common language where this error arises). This might help you understand the problem and, if you\'re a developer, fix the actual code that\'s causing the error.\n\n**Likely Cause:**\n\nThe error `TypeError(\'exceptions must derive from BaseException\')` in Python means that you\'re trying to raise or catch something as an exception that isn\'t a proper exception class (i.e., it doesn\'t inherit from `BaseException`, directly or indirectly).\n\n**Common Scenarios and Fixes:**\n\n1.  **Raising a Non-Exception Object:**\n\n    You might be trying to `raise` something that isn\'t an exception.  For example:\n\n    ```python\n    # Incorrect\n    raise "This is not an exception"\n\n    # Correct\n    raise ValueError("This is a ValueError exception")\n    ```\n\n    **Fix:** Ensure that you are raising an instance of a class that inherits from `BaseException` (e.g., `Exception`, `ValueError`, `TypeError`, `RuntimeError`, or a custom exception you\'ve defined).\n\n2.  **Catching a Non-Exception Object:**\n\n    Less common, but you might be trying to catch something that isn\'t an exception type in an `except` clause.\n\n    ```python\n    # Incorrect (this will not work as intended)\n    try:\n        # ... some code that might raise an exception ...\n        pass\n    except "This is not an exception":  # This is the problem\n        # ... handle the exception ...\n        pass\n\n    # Correct\n    try:\n        # ... some code that might raise an exception ...\n        pass\n    except ValueError:  # Catching a ValueError exception\n        # ... handle the exception ...\n        pass\n    except Exception as e: # Catching any exception and assigning it to e\n        # ... handle the exception ...\n        print(f"An error occurred: {e}")\n    ```\n\n    **Fix:**  Make sure your `except` clauses specify valid exception types (classes that inherit from `BaseException`).  It\'s often good practice to catch specific exception types when you know what to expect, and then have a general `except Exception as e:` clause to catch anything else.\n\n3.  **Custom Exceptions:**\n\n    If you\'ve defined your own exception classes, make sure they inherit from `Exception` (or another built-in exception class).\n\n    ```python\n    # Incorrect\n    class MyError:  # Doesn\'t inherit from BaseException\n        pass\n\n    # Correct\n    class MyError(Exception):  # Inherits from Exception\n        pass\n    ```\n\n    **Fix:** Ensure your custom exception classes inherit from `Exception` or another appropriate base exception class.\n\n**How this Relates to Me (the AI):**\n\nWhile I can\'t show you the exact code that caused the error in my system, the underlying problem is likely one of these three scenarios.  My code (written by the developers) probably tried to `raise` or `except` something that wasn\'t a valid exception type.\n\n**To Help Me (Indirectly):**\n\nThe *most helpful* thing you can do is provide the *exact query* that triggered the error, and the *history of the conversation* leading up to it.  This will allow the developers to reproduce the error and pinpoint the problematic code.  Without that information, it\'s very difficult to debug.\n\nI hope this explanation is helpful, even though I can\'t directly fix the code myself.  Please provide the query and conversation history if you can!', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={'prompt_feedback': {'block_reason': 0, 'safety_ratings': []}, 'finish_reason': 'STOP', 'model_name': 'gemini-2.0-flash', 'safety_ratings': []}, name='llm_expert', id='run--daba9878-eec2-4446-b7e5-1d915e32b917-0', usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 646, 'output_tokens': 867, 'total_tokens': 1513, 'input_token_details': {'cache_read': 0}}), AIMessage(content='The query "What is ai